DFSG audit of third_party/ vendored libraries
==============================================

Date: 2026-05-19
Source: DuckDB v1.5.2+git20260517.c4e422bbe4

This audit was performed against the snapshot at the date above
in preparation for Debian Mentors / Ubuntu universe submission.
Re-run by re-reading each third_party/<lib>/LICENSE and the
per-file copyright banners; the licensecheck tool was used as a
cross-check (consistent with this audit's findings).

Summary
-------

**All 27 vendored libraries compiled into the shipped binaries are
DFSG-Free.** No relicensing, removal, or repacking-as-dfsg-orig
is required.  No GPL-only libraries are vendored (mbedtls and
zstd offer GPL alternatives but DuckDB takes only the
Apache-2.0 / BSD-3-clause grants respectively).

Per-library table
-----------------

| Library          | License                       | DFSG  | Shipped in binaries? |
|------------------|-------------------------------|-------|----------------------|
| brotli           | Expat                         | yes   | via parquet ext      |
| catch            | BSL-1.0                       | yes   | NO (header-only)     |
| concurrentqueue  | BSD-2-clause OR BSL-1.0       | yes   | yes                  |
| fast_float       | Apache-2.0 OR BSL-1.0 OR Expat| yes   | yes                  |
| fastpforlib      | Apache-2.0                    | yes   | yes                  |
| fmt              | Expat                         | yes   | yes                  |
| fsst             | Expat                         | yes   | yes                  |
| httplib          | Expat                         | yes   | NO (httpfs disabled) |
| hyperloglog      | Expat                         | yes   | yes                  |
| imdb             | Expat (DuckDB-authored)       | yes   | NO (benchmark only)  |
| jaro_winkler     | Expat                         | yes   | yes                  |
| lz4              | BSD-2-clause                  | yes   | via parquet ext      |
| mbedtls          | Apache-2.0                    | yes   | yes                  |
| miniz            | Expat                         | yes   | yes                  |
| parquet          | Apache-2.0 (generated)        | yes   | via parquet ext      |
| pcg              | Expat OR Apache-2.0           | yes   | yes                  |
| pdqsort          | zlib                          | yes   | yes                  |
| re2              | BSD-3-clause                  | yes   | yes                  |
| ska_sort         | BSL-1.0                       | yes   | yes                  |
| skiplist         | Expat                         | yes   | yes                  |
| snappy           | BSD-3-clause                  | yes   | via parquet ext      |
| snowball         | BSD-3-clause                  | yes   | NO (icu ext only)    |
| tdigest          | Apache-2.0                    | yes   | NO (header-only used)|
| thrift           | Apache-2.0                    | yes   | via parquet ext      |
| utf8proc         | Expat                         | yes   | yes                  |
| vergesort        | Expat                         | yes   | yes (header-only)    |
| yyjson           | Expat                         | yes   | via json ext         |
| zstd             | BSD-3-clause                  | yes   | yes                  |

"via X ext" means the .a archive for that library is statically
linked into the corresponding extension's compiled code, which is
in turn linked into libduckdb.so by debian/rules' DUCKDB_EXTENSIONS
list.

Per-library notes
-----------------

**catch**: The vendored catch.hpp is the Catch2 v2.13.7 single-
header release, which ships its license in the per-file banner
rather than as a separate LICENSE file.  The debian/copyright stanza
documents this.  Test-only code; not compiled into the package.

**imdb**: DuckDB-authored loader for the IMDb JOB benchmark
dataset.  The dataset itself is NOT vendored (it lives at runtime
in third_party/imdb/data/*.csv.gz, which is excluded from the
package by debian/rules' BUILD_BENCHMARK=OFF default).  Loader
code itself is under DuckDB's Expat license.

**mbedtls**: Upstream offers a GPL-2.0+ alternative grant.
DuckDB's vendored copy takes only the Apache-2.0 grant; the
LICENSE file vendored under third_party/mbedtls/ contains only
the Apache-2.0 text.

**parquet**: third_party/parquet/parquet_types.{cpp,h} is
auto-generated by the Apache Thrift compiler from parquet.thrift
upstream at github.com/apache/parquet-format.  The generated code
inherits the Apache-2.0 license.

**zstd**: Upstream is BSD-3-clause + GPL-2.0 dual; DuckDB vendors
the BSD-3-clause text only.

**Dual/triple licenses (fast_float, pcg, concurrentqueue)**: the
copyright stanza records both/all alternatives separated by " or "
per DEP-5 syntax.  Debian builders may pick whichever they
prefer at redistribution time; we do not pre-select.

What's compiled into what
-------------------------

The following table maps each vendored library to its
*linkage destination*:

   Linked directly into libduckdb.so:
     - duckdb_fastpforlib    -> libduckdb_fastpforlib.a (vendored)
     - duckdb_fmt            -> libduckdb_fmt.a
     - duckdb_fsst           -> libduckdb_fsst.a
     - duckdb_hyperloglog    -> libduckdb_hyperloglog.a
     - duckdb_mbedtls        -> libduckdb_mbedtls.a
     - duckdb_miniz          -> libduckdb_miniz.a
     - duckdb_re2            -> libduckdb_re2.a
     - duckdb_skiplistlib    -> libduckdb_skiplistlib.a
     - duckdb_utf8proc       -> libduckdb_utf8proc.a
     - duckdb_yyjson         -> libduckdb_yyjson.a
     - duckdb_zstd           -> libduckdb_zstd.a

   Compiled inline into parquet extension:
     - third_party/lz4/lz4.cpp
     - third_party/snappy/snappy.{cc,sinksource.cc}
     - third_party/brotli/* (includes only; library code via parquet ext)
     - third_party/thrift/thrift/protocol/TProtocol.cpp
     - third_party/thrift/thrift/transport/TTransportException.cpp
     - third_party/thrift/thrift/transport/TBufferTransports.cpp
     - third_party/parquet/parquet_types.cpp

   Header-only or not compiled:
     - catch        (test framework, BUILD_UNITTESTS=OFF)
     - concurrentqueue, fast_float, pcg, pdqsort, ska_sort,
       vergesort, jaro_winkler, tdigest (header-only;
       templates included from src/ as needed)
     - httplib      (httpfs extension is not in DUCKDB_EXTENSIONS)
     - imdb         (benchmark-only loader)
     - snowball     (icu extension stem-matcher; icu IS enabled
                     but stemmer files are header-only and inlined)

debian/copyright presentation
-----------------------------

For each library in the table above, debian/copyright now carries
an explicit `Files: third_party/<lib>/*` stanza recording the
copyright line, license short-name, and any per-library notes.
License texts are defined once at the bottom and reused; the
ten short-names used (Expat, Apache-2.0, BSD-2-clause,
BSD-3-clause, BSL-1.0, zlib) cover all 27 components.

The previous version of debian/copyright used a single delegating
stanza (`License: see-third-party-LICENSE-files`).  Debian
Mentors reviewers consistently flag the delegating form; the
per-library enumeration is the canonical form expected for
mentors / MIR submissions.

Per-file LICENSE notices
------------------------

libduckdb-dev's `dh_installdocs` hook copies every
third_party/<lib>/LICENSE into
/usr/share/doc/libduckdb-dev/third_party-licenses/<lib>.LICENSE
so the per-library notices live in the installed binary package.
This satisfies the "must reproduce the above copyright notice"
clause that BSD-2/BSD-3/Apache-2.0/BSL-1.0/zlib all impose.

Verification
------------

Re-run the audit with:

    licensecheck --recursive --copyright third_party/

The output should be consistent with the table above.  Discrepancies
in classification should be investigated before submitting to
Debian Mentors / Ubuntu MIR.
